    -- fallback path. The lab's RESTORE field says which mode is actually live.
    RageRestoreMode      = "auto",
    -- ★ Polar seam S35 — CAMERA ANCHOR. The visible teleport is the CAMERA following the parked root part,
    -- not the body needing to move back. CameraController writes Camera.CFrame absolutely from the subject's
    -- position every frame at priority 200; we bind at 205 and add the displacement straight back. The root
    -- part stays parked for 100% of the frame, so this cannot cost power and cannot leak — the camera is not
    -- replicated (only the look ANGLE is, and we never touch rotation). Does NOT restore movement.
    RageCameraAnchor     = true,
    -- ★★★ Polar seam S37 — THE PART GLUE. Kicia's mechanism, transplanted exactly, and the only thing that
    -- buys POWER AND MOVEMENT AND no visible teleport at the same time. Per frame, with a target:
    -- redirect our root's PhysicsRepRootPart at the target's HitboxHead, break that hitbox's WeldConstraint
    -- and anchor it, drag it to a per-session rendezvous ~100k studs out, park our own body beside it, and
    -- restore the body before the render pass. The restore is FREE here — the server resolves us from the
    -- hitbox, so the restored pose is not what replicates. That is the whole reason Kicia can restore
    -- unconditionally and we could not.
    -- Turning this ON forces the restore to "kicia" for as long as the glue is live, because the two are one
    -- mechanism: park at the rendezvous with no restore and you are simply standing 100k studs away.
    -- ⚠ S36's own-part version of this is DELETED, not disabled. A part made by Instance.new on the client
    -- has no network identity, so the physics sender has nothing to serialize when it resolves through the
    -- redirect. Kicia's own source says so by omission: all ten of its Instance.new("Part") sites are
    -- cosmetic, and eight developers took on weld-breaking and character mutation when three lines would
    -- have done. Nobody pays that price when the cheap version works.
    -- ⚠ THIS WRITES TO ANOTHER PLAYER'S CHARACTER (local writes only — we do not own those parts, so nothing
    -- replicates). Every field is saved before the first write and restored on release. BURNER ONLY.
    -- The glue encoder remains experimental. A successful local binding does not prove that the server
    -- accepts its sentinel packet, so the live build stays on the measured body-park transport.
    RagePartGlue         = false,
    -- ★ @polarcore-seam:S44 GUM — the part glue, as one named mode: "off" | "lite" | "on".
    -- Renamed from RageGlueMode on operator instruction 2026-08-14; "full" became "on". The legacy key and
    -- the legacy boolean are both still honoured by gumMode() in 07c, so a saved config written before the
    -- rename cannot silently go inert. Added after re-reading both winners' sources line by line
    -- (memory: part-glue-verified-2026-08-14).
    --   "on"   = RagePartGlue above, unchanged: Kicia's PhysicsGlue verbatim — redirect, break the hitbox's
    --            WeldConstraint, anchor it, drag it to a per-session rendezvous ~100k studs out, park our
    --            body 0.7 studs away, and send the S38 -9e37 sentinel packet. BURNER ONLY: it mutates
    --            another player's character, a write class nothing else in this bundle performs.
    --   "lite" = UE's shape, and it is strictly smaller: the sethiddenproperty redirect and NOTHING else.
    --            No weld surgery, no anchor, no hitbox move, no rendezvous, and the body park and the wire
    --            format stay exactly what the measured build already ships. ONE reversible property write.
    --            WHY THIS EXISTS: `WeldConstraint`, `Part1`, `Part0`, `Weld` and `Motor6D` appear ZERO times
    --            in the whole UE dump, and UE's ragebot subtree never touches Anchored/Parent/Destroy. UE
    --            wins with the redirect alone, so Kicia's surgery is an ADDITION, not a requirement — and
    --            this is the cheapest honest probe of whether the redirect by itself moves anything.
    --   "off"  = shipped default. Zero change from the build that beats Kicia.
    -- ⚠ NO KEY MAY BE INERT. That failure has already cost this project days twice: `RageRestoreMode
    -- = "auto"` silently resolving to "none" made the whole translocate seam dead code, and a parity veto
    -- above a config read made three ON-by-default GUI toggles do nothing. So gumMode() in 07c is written
    -- so that ALL THREE keys work: this string wins when it is one of the three valid values; failing that
    -- the legacy `RageGlueMode` is read and its "full" maps to "on"; failing that `RagePartGlue = true`
    -- still promotes to "on". Whatever it resolves to is printed by Rage._gumDiag() — check that before
    -- believing any glue result.
    RageGumMode          = "off",
    -- ★ @polarcore-seam:S45 VOID PREFIRE — the reason we lose to UE, fixed. Requires RageGumMode = "on".
    -- Memory: why-we-lose-to-ue-daydreamer-2026-08-14.
    -- WHAT WAS WRONG: 07c's `if not isSanePos(hpos) then return hide(...)` bail sits ABOVE the glue, and
    -- SANE_POS_LIMIT is 100000 while UE voids to 2.1e9. So every single UE hide made us abandon the attack
    -- path entirely — no glue, no fire. That is the whole scoreboard: we beat every script whose coordinate
    -- stays sane (Kicia rendezvouses at ~1e5) and lose to the one that does not.
    -- WHY THE OLD BAIL WAS WRONG, twice over: (1) the game's own SHARED source says target validity is
    -- `IsAlive() + enemy TeamID + same EnvironmentID` (EntityLookupParams:17, GameplayUtility:441) with NO
    -- positional term, so a voided enemy is still in the raycast whitelist; (2) daydreamer and Kicia both
    -- fire at voided hitboxes and both beat UE.
    -- HOW: when the coordinate is unusable we stop trusting COORDINATES and use IDENTITY. glueAcquire takes
    -- the HitboxHead *instance* and drags it to our rendezvous (~1e5, comfortably inside the 2^22 spatial
    -- limit) — which is exactly why this needs "on" and not "lite": "lite" has no drag, so our replication
    -- root would sit at 2.1e9 and blow that limit. daydreamer's partglue:acquire drags for the same reason.
    -- BOUNDED: only inside S17's existing `aboutToResurface` window — [median - 0.15s, median + 0.15s] of a
    -- learned absence, and never below PRED_NEEDED = 3 samples. A wrong prediction still costs at most
    -- 2 x PRED_LEAD once per absence, exactly as it did before. This does NOT fire through a whole hide;
    -- daydreamer's own gate unblocks only for the last Stability = 0.15s of a learned absence and that is
    -- the behaviour being matched.
    -- ⛔ NO GUI CONTROL since 2026-08-15 (operator: "no point of it"). Only the toggle in the Rage tab was
    -- removed; this default and every reader in 07c are unchanged, so the behaviour is exactly what it was
    -- with the toggle left on. It now sits under the same no-rage-internals rule as RagePerfectRestore and
    -- the rest: a config saved while the toggle existed still carries the key, which is harmless because
    -- LinoriaLib only writes Config through a control's Callback -- no control, no write.
    RageGumVoidFire      = true,
    -- ★ @polarcore-seam:S39 THE BAIT — Kicia's single largest rage advantage, and it is OFFENSE.
    -- The operator flips Evasion Random -> Translocate -> Random the instant they see their own bot firing,
    -- once or twice a fight, and their ragebot then beats every competing ragebot. The reason, from source
    -- plus the operator's own description ("it teleports u to some kill plane and if someone follows u
    -- there they die"): Kicia's compute (L141596-141600) returns
    --     part.CFrame * CFrame.new(0, -part.Size.Y/2 + Evasion.Translocate.Offset, 0)
    -- over the first CollectionService "OutOfBoundsPart" with KillDelay == 0 — an INSTANT kill volume —
    -- with Offset = -5 (L61039). Five studs under the bottom face: safely outside, hard against it.
    -- WHY IT WINS. While firing, a ragebot's replicated body sits ON the enemy's head — precisely where an
    -- OPPOSING ragebot's park converges, because they park on our body too. The flip evacuates our body to
    -- the lip of an instant-kill volume; anything still locked to it follows it in and DIES. Flipping back
    -- resumes the burst with the attack state intact (Kicia clears _attackStart only inside _Plan, which
    -- the Translocate branch skips), so the dwell is already paid. One or two flips is enough because each
    -- one can delete an opposing ragebot outright. It reads as "hits far more, gets hit far less" because
    -- the opponent is dead and we are firing at someone who is respawning.
    -- ⚠ WHY IT WAS OFF, and why that reasoning was wrong. The build gate recorded "root-only Translocate
    -- was measured to cost attacks without adding defense" (build.js:635-637) — literally true, and the
    -- wrong question: it adds NO defense, it kills the opponent, and no counter in this bundle was watching
    -- for that. It was then gated behind RagePartGlue on a theory that the glue was what made it free. The
    -- operator ran the glue: rage broke. Kicia uses no glue whatsoever on this path. Both errors corrected.
    -- ⚠ HONEST RISK. Our park is a PHYSICAL body (restoreMode "none" suppresses both anchors mid-burst),
    -- so a destination even slightly inside the volume kills US instead. That is exactly what Offset = -5
    -- is for, it is Kicia's own shipped number, and 07c refuses the pulse when no zero-delay part exists.
    -- Watch RageTranslocateBaits vs deaths in the lab; if we die on pulse frames, make the offset MORE
    -- negative rather than disabling this.
    -- ⚠ HISTORY, so nobody re-litigates this from the symptom. Enabling this on 2026-08-11 coincided with
    -- "rage is broken doesn't attack or do anything", and I blamed THIS seam and reverted it. That was
    -- wrong: the operator reported the break persisted with this key false. The actual cause was a deleted
    -- `_inMatch = inMatch()` in 07c's Heartbeat (lost while inserting the S41 canary) — _inMatch stayed
    -- false forever, so every frame returned early as "Lobby" regardless of any setting here. Two lessons:
    -- a symptom that survives the revert was never caused by the thing reverted, and "it broke when I
    -- enabled X" is correlation until X is proven to run at all.
    -- The physical-body risk below is still real, but it is now handled structurally rather than by
    -- shipping this off: 07c gates the pulse on restoreMode() ~= "none", the only transport where our real
    -- body is restored every RenderStep the way Kicia's is. Under "none" the seam is skipped, not dangerous.
    -- ⛔⛔ FLIPPED true -> false ON 2026-08-14, AND IT IS A NO-OP FOR CURRENT BEHAVIOUR. Read this before
    -- flipping it back. 07c:1953 gates the pulse on `restoreMode() ~= "none"`, and restoreMode() (07c:494)
    -- returns "none" for every shipped configuration: RageRestoreMode is "auto" (falls through), the glue
    -- is off, and 13_gui registers NO control for either key. So this seam has never fired a single pulse
    -- in any build the operator has run — State.RageTranslocateBaits has always read 0.
    -- THE REASON IT HAD TO MOVE: `RagePartGlue = true` makes glueActive() true, which makes restoreMode()
    -- return "kicia", which OPENS this gate. So the one and only glue soak silently enabled this seam too —
    -- and this seam is the one independently measured as "rage is broken doesn't attack or do anything".
    -- Both failures plausibly had one cause and it was not the glue. Leaving this true would mean any glue
    -- test still changes three things at once and remains unreadable. #174.
    -- ⇒ To test the bait, set this true AND RageRestoreMode to "kicia" or "render" deliberately, alone.
    -- ⇒ To test the glue, leave this false. That is the whole point of the change.
    RageAttackTranslocate = false,
    -- ⛔ RageTranslocateDwell (0.22s hold) EXISTED HERE AND WAS DELETED 2026-08-12. It was built on the
    -- theory that a pursuing ragebot had to follow us into the volume and die there, which needed a
    -- 60-150ms round trip of dwell. The operator falsified the premise: "if kicia in translocate even we
    -- can attack it, its not that being in translocate is unhittable, thats not the mechanism" — the
    -- volume is not cover, so a dwell only adds exposure and lost fire time. The payload is the one-frame
    -- DISPLACEMENT (see above), not the destination. Do not reintroduce a hold.
    --
    -- ⛔⛔ @polarcore-seam:S43 ZERO CYCLE — BUILT AND FULLY REVERTED ON 2026-08-12. READ THIS BEFORE
    -- REBUILDING IT. It ran the S42 pulse as a continuous duty cycle (RageZeroCycle + RageZeroCyclePeriod),
    -- always to Kicia's kill plane, plus a 0.35s "hide lure" at the head of each reload hide. It also
    -- carried three genuine findings that are worth keeping even though the code is gone:
    --   · Kicia's compute has a THIRD branch (cleaned L141593-141594): with a target but no KillDelay == 0
    --     OutOfBoundsPart it returns a random far point, not nil. Ours returned nil, so the seam was
    --     silently inert on any map without a kill volume.
    --   · The `restoreMode() ~= "none"` gate on this seam protects NOTHING. PolarCore.start's Stepped
    --     handler calls reparkAfterRender() under "render", so the body is at the park across the physics
    --     step in BOTH modes. The gate only ever chose between two equally exposed transports.
    --   · Kicia's SpatialLimitGate trips at 2^22 studs, and the kill plane is an ordinary map coordinate
    --     far below that — so a lured ragebot is never classified as "target hidden" and does follow.
    -- ⛔ WHY IT CAME OUT: the operator measured the build WEAKER THAN BASELINE **WITH THE TOGGLE OFF**, in
    -- a matchup we had been winning, and weaker again with it on. Nothing in S43 executed with the toggle
    -- off — every entry point was behind zeroCycle() or behind the pre-existing restoreMode gate — so the
    -- regression has NO IDENTIFIED MECHANISM. That is precisely why the whole seam was reverted rather than
    -- narrowed down: an unexplained regression sitting on the attack path is not something to bisect in
    -- place. Do not re-add any part of this until the regression itself is explained.
    --
    -- (historical description of what it did, kept for whoever rebuilds it)
    -- ★★★ ZERO CYCLE — the same pulse, run continuously instead of twice a burst.
    -- Operator request, 2026-08-12: "when we attack we also translocate every time — this isnt just one
    -- swap, its swapping between translocate and attack very fast (but still attack should land). The goal
    -- is that when enemy ragebot is following us and we are attacking and suddenly go to translocate, enemy
    -- could be slow to find that we are in translocate state and follow us and die to kill plane OR enemy
    -- could mess up their shots." Both payoffs are real and they are independent:
    --   · FOLLOW-AND-DIE needs the kill-plane destination and a pursuer whose park lags ours by a network
    --     round trip. Bounded by their reaction, not by us.
    --   · SHOT CORRUPTION is the S42 predictor poison and needs only the displacement. Every pulse lands it
    --     on every enemy tracking us, whatever they do.
    -- S42 fires this twice a burst because that is a human's limit on a dropdown. We have no such limit, so
    -- Zero Cycle lifts the cap and runs it as a duty cycle. It IMPLIES RageAttackTranslocate (07c's
    -- translocateEnabled) so a saved config carrying that master false cannot make this toggle inert.
    --
    -- ⚠ WHAT IT COSTS, EXACTLY, because "but still attack should land" is the constraint. A translocate
    -- frame is never a shot origin — the server resolves our rounds from the REPLICATED body, and on a
    -- pulse frame that body is ~1e5 studs from the target — so the cycle spends one frame in
    -- (period + 1) and we keep the rest of the burst. At the default 8 that is 89% of our packets; at the
    -- hard floor of 3 it is still 75%. 07c clamps the period to 3..20 and BOTH ends are load-bearing —
    -- simulated, not guessed. Floor 3: below it this is not a fast cycle, it is a permanent evacuation that
    -- never lands anything. Ceiling 20: a magazine is ~30 shooting frames at 1 tap/frame and the arm
    -- counter resets on every reload hide, so a period of 30 fires ZERO pulses per burst — the slider's own
    -- top end would have made the feature silently inert.
    -- Shipped keys were RageZeroCycle (false) and RageZeroCyclePeriod (8, clamped 3..20 in 07c: below 3 it
    -- is a permanent evacuation that never lands a shot, at 20+ the period exceeds a magazine and it never
    -- pulses at all). A third, RageZeroCycleKillPlane, was removed by the operator the same day — "obv
    -- cycle to kill plane what else?" — because the void variant can poison a predictor but can never kill
    -- anybody. All three keys are GONE, not merely defaulted off. A LinoriaLib config saved on 2026-08-12
    -- may still contain them; with no control registered they are inert on load.
    -- ⇒ END OF S43 TOMBSTONE.
    -- ★ Polar seam S29 — taps per frame. Kicia sends exactly ONE attack per frame; POLAR_TAPS_SANE = 6 is
    -- the standalone's number and stays the upper clamp. Accepted packets are measured invariant to volume,
    -- so 2..6 spend the magazine 6x faster for nothing. Raise back toward 6 only to A/B against this.
    RageTapsPerFrame     = 1,
    -- Polar S11: in an HvH lobby, shoot the player who can actually kill us before the lowest-HP bot.
    RagePrioritizeHackers = true,
    -- Polar S12: 0-250ms random extra hide so our void length stops being a learnable constant. Costs
    -- ~5%% uptime; the spread has to beat a 0.15s predictor lead to be worth anything at all.
    RageHideJitter        = true,
    -- Polar seam S8: withhold the burst until the park has had a one-way trip to replicate. Costs ~2
    -- frames at the start of a burst; buys back the 40-60% of the magazine that is currently fired from
    -- a body position the server has not received yet. Wait is derived from live ping, not tuned here.
    -- 07c no longer HIDES on a protected target: it targets and parks on the head exactly as it would on a
    -- killable one, and withholds only the packets (rejected by construction while IsInvincible is set). The
    -- old skip un-parked us, so when the protection cleared we were 110-140k studs away and had to re-acquire
    -- and re-teleport first -- and that travel time is the whole HvH margin. Protected enemies also sort
    -- strictly below killable ones, so they can never starve us of a real target. In 05_utils this key still
    -- gates non-rage validation (aimbot / silent aim / ESP do not lock invincible players).
    RageHPPriority       = true,   -- prefer the lowest-HP target (else nearest)
    RageFastTargetSwitch = true,   -- drop a target the instant it becomes invalid
    RageVisCheck         = false,  -- require LOS to acquire (findTarget); off = point-blank guarantees LOS anyway

    -- (Weapon economy is gone: 07c fires whatever is equipped and always reloads-and-hides when empty,
    -- which is the standalone's behaviour and the winning one. Picking the weapon was never ours anyway.)

    -- Backstab geometry (the only remaining rage raycasts — flank a deflect/shield front arc). ORBIT only:
    -- Polar always parks point-blank on the head, the geometry the standalone proved.
    AvoidDeflect       = true,   -- get behind a katana user (its deflect only guards the front)
    RageShieldBackstab = true,   -- get behind a riot-shield user (its block only guards the front)
    RageKnifeBackstab  = true,   -- when WE hold a knife, prefer the backstab spot

    RageKillPlaneBuffer = 200,   -- studs above FallenPartsDestroyHeight we clamp the point-blank park to
    -- (The kill-plane clamp is now UNCONDITIONAL inside 07c, as in the standalone -- no switch to get wrong.)

    RageLab = false,             -- in-game telemetry overlay (11_lab)

    RageVoidPhase = true,  -- LIVE: 11_lab reads this as the hide-detection gate (keep true; NOT dead)

    -- ── RAGE MODE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- A saved config naming a deleted mode ("Apex"/"Phantom") falls through to Polar, which is correct.
    RageMode = "Polar",              -- "Polar" | "Orbit" (dropdown; default = the live-proven Polar)

    -- true (DEFAULT) = FORGED burst: we build + FireServer the UseItem packet RageTaps times, each tap with
    -- freshly jittered char3. false = NATURAL: ask the game via lf:Input and let it build + send, our 08 hook
    -- rewriting its camData in place. Delivery is NOT the ban vector (proven by the 2026-07-27 soak) — the
    -- packet SHAPE was, and both paths now share buildShotFields. Forged is default for the burst damage.
    RageDirectFire        = true,
    -- BULLET ECONOMY (2026-07-29). The forged burst has NO rate gate: it ships RageTaps packets every
    -- Heartbeat (~360/sec at 60fps). The gate at 07b:253 guards only the NATURAL path, and RageLastFireTime
    -- is written only by 07a, so it is stale on the forged path regardless. The server rejects most of the
    -- spam, but every packet it ACCEPTS costs a round -- so if it accepts more than one per weapon interval
    -- the mag drains far faster than the gun allows, forcing extra reloads (and reload = void = another
    -- first-bullet-of-the-mag loss). Turning this ON limits the forged burst to one burst per weapon fire
    -- interval. DEFAULT OFF so shipped burst damage is unchanged until it is A/B'd live: if the server does
    -- accept multiple taps per interval, this cuts burst DPS, which is the wrong trade against Kicia.
    RageRateLimit         = false,
    RageTaps              = 6,

    -- ORBIT tuning (anti-normal-player stepped side-vantage rager)
    RageCombatOrbitRadius = 60,      -- base vantage distance
    -- ★ 0.09 -> 0.30 (2026-08-07). The comment "replication settle window" had the right idea and the
    -- wrong number: one stock physics-sender period is 1/15 s = 0.067, so a 0.09 dwell left ~23ms of
    -- actual firing per vantage and spent the rest at a position the server had not received yet. Orbit
    -- now withholds fire for ORBIT_PRIME_S after every vantage jump, so the dwell has to be long enough
    -- for that to leave a usable window: 0.30 gives ~77% firing time against 0.09's ~25%.
    RageOrbitDwell        = 0.30,    -- seconds each vantage is held; must exceed 1/15s or Orbit never lands
    RageCombatOrbitHeight = 8,       -- vantage height above the head
    RageCombatOrbitJitter = true,    -- +/- vertical spread per candidate
    RagePBEyeUp           = 3,       -- eye height above the vantage (Orbit reads this; Polar hardcodes +2.5)

